{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun5/packet.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun5/packet.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage A reading form: is each answer a sound judged question for its thesis, or a justified refusal? (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs. Do not read any other reader's answers.\n\n## What you are reading\n\nThe packet you are given holds a list of items. Each item is one research topic (\"thesis\") a system opened\nwhile reading one news article, with a proposed **judged question** for it:\n- `thesis`: the fields the system wrote when it opened the topic: `description` (the topic's wording), `outcome` (what is tracked),\n  `period`, `opens_as`, `settled_by`;\n- `article`: the opening article's publication date, title and full text;\n- `ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it`: the proposed judged question exactly as a later judge will see it: its `object` (the company's\n  ticker NVDA, or a theme id), `kind`, `question`, `year`, `due_day` and its `rungs` (each a label and a condition; exactly one rung\n  is meant to hold on the due day). Or `no_ladder` with a reason: the step declined to write a question;\n- `other_fields`: what the step recorded beside the ladder and the judge does not see: `not_judged` (parts of the topic's wording\n  the question leaves out, with why), `reference` (what the article says the market expects, quoted), `favourable_end`,\n  `due_day_basis`.\n\nSome items are sound and some carry a fault. Judge each on its own against its thesis and its article.\n\n## The rules a judged question must meet\n\n- One judged question asks **one** figure for a named period or **one** event: the one the thesis's `outcome` names. Every other\n  part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) must be listed\n  in `not_judged`.\n- A yes/no event has two rungs, Yes and No; an event with more states, or a figure, has five. Exactly one rung holds for every\n  possible outcome: no overlap, no gap.\n- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is the company's (object NVDA,\n  kind asset). A question about what happens in the world (licensing, aggregate spending, a policy) is a theme's.\n- A company's five-rung ladder runs from Strongly bullish to Strongly bearish, meaning good or bad for the company's business.\n- The reference is what the market expects for the question when it opens: a consensus, the company's guidance, a stated plan or\n  deadline. A figure's middle rung is the outcome near the reference.\n- The due day is when the question can be resolved: a figure's publication date; for an event, the date its outcome is assessed. Where\n  the thesis or the article states the day a figure is published, that day is right; where neither does, a plausible reporting\n  date after the period ends is right.\n- A step may decline (`no_ladder`) only in the five cases its instructions allow: the thesis's outcome names several things and\n  none is the main one; nothing in it can be checked on a date; it is the company's figure for a quarter or a half-year; it is a\n  figure for a year that had already ended on the article's day; or it is a figure for a year beyond next year, unless the thesis's\n  `opens_as` is binding_obligation. Declining for any other reason, or when the case named does not truly apply, is not justified.\n\n## What to return\n\nOne JSON object per item, one per line, in item order, as your final message: no prose before or after, no code fence. Fields:\n\n| field | values | the mark |\n|---|---|---|\n| `item` | the item number | |\n| `Q` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the judged question asks the thing the thesis's `outcome` names (`n/a` for a `no_ladder`) |\n| `O` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the object is the question's own company or the right theme |\n| `R` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | exactly one rung holds for every outcome; for a yes/no, the Yes condition is the event happening and the No condition its complement |\n| `D` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the due day follows the rule for its kind. A figure: a day by which the figure can be expected to have been published, after its period ends (the day the thesis or the article states, or, where neither states one, a plausible reporting date). An event: the date the thesis or the article gives for assessing it, or the last day of the period it names, or the company's fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision with no date. It fails when the day contradicts a date the thesis or the article gives, falls before the figure's period ends, or, for an event, is a date none of these gives |\n| `X` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | where the article states what the market expects for this quantity or event, the ladder places it rightly (a figure: in the middle rung); where the article states none, it holds unless the ladder claims one |\n| `F` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a five-rung ladder about the company only: the direction is defensible (which end is bullish, or the labels of the states); `n/a` for a yes/no ladder and for a theme |\n| `Y` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a figure only: the year and the unit are the question's |\n| `G` | `holds`, `fails`, `not_assessable`, `n/a` | for a figure only: where the article gives any figure for this quantity, an analyst would accept the ranges as a fair division of the outcomes around it; `not_assessable` when the article gives none |\n| `L` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | every other part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) is listed in `not_judged`; a wording that asks nothing else holds with an empty list |\n| `B` | list | for each part listed in `not_judged`, in its order: `bet` when an analyst would hold a separate view on it, `not_a_bet` when it is a detail of the judged question; `[]` when nothing is listed |\n| `XR` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the `reference` field quotes what the article states the market expects, with the right number, and is null only when the article states none |\n| `N` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a `no_ladder` only: the refusal is justified by one of the allowed cases |\n| `W` | `\"\"` or a short sentence | any other fault that would mislead a reader of this judged question; empty when none |\n| `why` | one sentence | the reason for every `fails`, naming the words or the figure |\n\nRead each item's article before answering it. Judge only what is in the item.\n[\n {\n  \"item\": 1,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will France's competition authority reach a conclusion/decision — a fine, behavioral remedies or closure — in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027)?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's decision concluding its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA, including any fine or behavioral remedies imposed.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item gives no date for the conclusion, only that it is 'nearing a conclusion'.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's formal decision/announcement concluding its investigation, and NVIDIA's related disclosure of any fine or remedies.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks Beijing's decision on whether to allow H200 sales into mainland China; this tracks a European antitrust decision on NVIDIA's cloud-AI/GPU conduct. They are different regulators, jurisdictions and subject matter and can move apart — China can grant (or keep barring) H200 access while France imposes remedies, or the reverse.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"What will France's competition authority have decided in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA by 2027-01-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has closed the investigation with no finding of an infringement and without accepting commitments from NVIDIA.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has closed the investigation with no finding of an infringement, accepting commitments from NVIDIA.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 3,\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has made no final decision; procedural steps alone count here.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 4,\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has found an infringement and ordered no remedy of its own, whether or not it imposed a fine.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 5,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has found an infringement and ordered a remedy of its own, whether or not it imposed a fine.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"period_end\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 2,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How far will the US crackdown on advanced AI-chip exports to third countries escalate — will the US Commerce Department go beyond its May 2026 guidance and impose formal licensing requirements or other escalated restrictions on AI-chip shipments to third countries such as Malaysia, Singapore and Japan by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security moves beyond its May 2026 guidance to a binding export-licensing rule or other escalated restriction on AI-chip shipments to third countries, and the resulting constraint on Nvidia's sales to those markets.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set by the reason: a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"A US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security Federal Register rule, licence requirement or formal export-control action.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 turns on Beijing's decision whether to allow Chinese commercial buyers to purchase H200 chips the US cleared in December 2025; this thesis turns on Washington's decision whether to tighten third-country diversion controls. The two can move independently — Beijing could reopen H200 purchases while Washington hardens third-country rules, or Washington could act while Beijing stays closed.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia halves Asia buyer list in China chip crackdown, steps up due diligence: report\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\\nOver the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter.\\nReuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours.\\nThe tighter scrutiny comes as the Trump administration seeks to prevent advanced US chips from reaching Chinese entities through third countries.\\nThe US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\\nNvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\\nThe US Department of Commerce is also involved, providing oversight and political backing, the report said.\\nThe department did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours. REUTERS\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"chip-export-controls\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\",\n   \"question\": \"How far will US export restrictions on advanced AI-chip shipments to third countries have gone by 2027-01-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Guidance eased, no rule\",\n     \"condition\": \"No binding licensing rule is proposed or in force, and the May 2026 guidance has been withdrawn or relaxed.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"Guidance kept, no rule\",\n     \"condition\": \"No binding licensing rule is proposed or in force, and the May 2026 guidance has not been withdrawn or relaxed.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 3,\n     \"label\": \"Rule proposed\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule has been proposed, and none is in force.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 4,\n     \"label\": \"Rule in force, partial\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule is in force for shipments to a named list of third countries.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 5,\n     \"label\": \"Rule in force, general\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule is in force, and it covers every third country outside an exempt list.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"period_end\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 3,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"What will the competition regulator decide on the grocer's takeover of its rival by its 2027-03-31 deadline?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The regulator's decision on the takeover by its deadline.\",\n   \"period\": \"The regulator's statutory deadline, 2027-03-31.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The regulator's decision notice.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"publisher\": \"(written for this dry run)\",\n   \"title\": \"Regulator opens in-depth review of grocery takeover\",\n   \"text\": \"The competition regulator opened an in-depth review of the grocer's takeover of its rival and set a statutory deadline of March 31, 2027. It can clear the deal, clear it with conditions or block it; the companies may appeal a block.\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"GRCR\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"What will the competition regulator have decided on the grocer's takeover of its rival by 2027-03-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-03-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The regulator has cleared the takeover with no conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The regulator has cleared the takeover with conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 3,\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"The regulator has made no decision, including a filing withdrawn.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 4,\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The regulator has blocked the takeover, and an appeal has been lodged.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 5,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The regulator has blocked the takeover, and no appeal has been lodged.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 4,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will France's competition authority reach a conclusion/decision — a fine, behavioral remedies or closure — in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA's alleged anti-competitive practices in the cloud-AI and GPU markets by NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027)?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The French competition authority's decision concluding its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA, including any fine or behavioral remedies imposed.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; the item gives no date for the conclusion, only that it is 'nearing a conclusion'.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"The French competition authority's formal decision/announcement concluding its investigation, and NVIDIA's related disclosure of any fine or remedies.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-3 tracks Beijing's decision on whether to allow H200 sales into mainland China; this tracks a European antitrust decision on NVIDIA's cloud-AI/GPU conduct. They are different regulators, jurisdictions and subject matter and can move apart — China can grant (or keep barring) H200 access while France imposes remedies, or the reverse.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-09\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Bears Add to Wagers as Competition Threatens Its Dominance: Short Seller Tracker\",\n   \"text\": \"Meta Says AI Compute Isn’t Enough: Is the Infrastructure Selloff Over?\\n5.3M Views · 140 Posts\\n$NVIDIA (NVDA.US)$ faced mounting pressure after bears added to their wagers against the chip giant amid increasing competition that threatened to challenge its dominance in the market for semiconductors that power artificial intelligence workloads.\\nTrading in borrowed Nvidia shares that were sold short rose to 13.61 million shares Wednesday, from 11.69 million shares a day earlier, exchange data tracked by moomoo showed. The latest figure represented 9.2% of the shares that changed hands yesterday, when the stock advanced 3.7%, the biggest gain in five weeks. Shorting involves shares sold without the seller owning them, which is a common practice used to profit from a potential decline in a stock's price.\\n$Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo. The social media giant that’s looking to compete with $Amazon (AMZN.US)$ , $Microsoft (MSFT.US)$ and $Alphabet-A (GOOGL.US)$ in providing AI cloud services, is working with $Broadcom (AVGO.US)$ to design and $Taiwan Semiconductor (TSM.US)$ to manufacture the AI chips, according to the report.\\nMeta is Nvidia’s fifth largest customer, behind Microsoft, Amazon, $Super Micro Computer (SMCI.US)$ and Alphabet, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\nStill, sales of Nvidia’s Taiwanese assembly partners climbed in June, signaling continued strong demand. Quanta, which supplies AI servers powered by Nvidia’s GPUs, saw its monthly sales more than double to 385.19 billion New Taiwanese dollars in June from a year earlier, according to data compiled by Bloomberg.\\n$HON HAI PRECISION INDUSTRY SPON GDS EACH REP 2 SHS TWD10 REG'S' (HNHPF.US)$ , Nvidia’s server assembly partner, saw a 52% surge in monthly sales in June to NT$ 821.8 billion, allowing the Taiwanese company to post a bigger-than-expected 40% surge in quarterly sales, according to Bloomberg.\\nNVIDIA recently issued a statement to Bloomberg denying claims of a technical delay, confirming that its product roadmap remains intact. This clarification serves as a counterweight to concerns that infrastructure bottlenecks could disrupt the company’s competitive lead in the artificial intelligence sector.\\nThe stock is also navigating a complex geopolitical environment as China moves to grant limited approval for domestic firms to purchase H200 processors. While this policy shift eases access to Nvidia’s technology, companies must now request individual approval for specific quantities and use cases.\\nRegulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. This probe focuses on allegations of anti-competitive practices within the cloud-AI and GPU markets.\\nShare your thoughts on Nvidia in the comments section. Are retail investors losing their faith in Nvidia? Let your voice be heard by voting below.\\nComments (4)\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"What will France's competition authority have decided in its antitrust investigation into NVIDIA by 2027-01-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has closed the investigation with no finding of an infringement and without accepting commitments from NVIDIA.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has closed the investigation, accepting commitments from NVIDIA or finding no infringement.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 3,\n     \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has made no final decision; procedural steps alone count here.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 4,\n     \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has found an infringement and ordered no remedy of its own, whether or not it imposed a fine.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 5,\n     \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n     \"condition\": \"The authority has found an infringement and ordered a remedy of its own, whether or not it imposed a fine.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"period_end\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 5,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"How far will the US crackdown on advanced AI-chip exports to third countries escalate — will the US Commerce Department go beyond its May 2026 guidance and impose formal licensing requirements or other escalated restrictions on AI-chip shipments to third countries such as Malaysia, Singapore and Japan by Nvidia's FY2027 year-end?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security moves beyond its May 2026 guidance to a binding export-licensing rule or other escalated restriction on AI-chip shipments to third countries, and the resulting constraint on Nvidia's sales to those markets.\",\n   \"period\": \"Nvidia's FY2027 year-end (late January 2027) — set by the reason: a pending regulatory decision is tracked to the current fiscal year-end.\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"policy_stage\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"A US Commerce Department / Bureau of Industry and Security Federal Register rule, licence requirement or formal export-control action.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-5 turns on Beijing's decision whether to allow Chinese commercial buyers to purchase H200 chips the US cleared in December 2025; this thesis turns on Washington's decision whether to tighten third-country diversion controls. The two can move independently — Beijing could reopen H200 purchases while Washington hardens third-country rules, or Washington could act while Beijing stays closed.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia halves Asia buyer list in China chip crackdown, steps up due diligence: report\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA has more than halved the number of Asian customers authorised to buy its AI chips after creating a new “white list” of companies that have passed tougher compliance checks aimed at preventing the products from reaching China, the Financial Times reported on Monday (Jul 14).\\nOver the past few months, the chipmaker has intensified due diligence in Singapore, Malaysia and Japan, the report added, citing three people familiar with the matter.\\nReuters could not immediately verify the report. Nvidia did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours.\\nThe tighter scrutiny comes as the Trump administration seeks to prevent advanced US chips from reaching Chinese entities through third countries.\\nThe US Commerce Department issued guidance in May aimed at curbing advanced AI chips from reaching overseas subsidiaries of Chinese companies, highlighting concerns that Nvidia’s cutting-edge Blackwell processors may have been exported to Chinese-linked entities in countries such as Malaysia despite US restrictions.\\nNvidia has tightened compliance procedures following pressure from Washington, the FT said, adding that staff now visit customers’ data centres, verify contracts and interview end users as part of the checks.\\nThe US Department of Commerce is also involved, providing oversight and political backing, the report said.\\nThe department did not immediately respond to a Reuters request for comment outside regular business hours. REUTERS\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"chip-export-controls\",\n   \"kind\": \"theme\",\n   \"question\": \"How far will US export restrictions on advanced AI-chip shipments to third countries have gone by 2027-01-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-01-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Guidance eased, no rule\",\n     \"condition\": \"No binding licensing rule is proposed or in force, and the May 2026 guidance has been withdrawn or relaxed.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"Guidance kept, no rule\",\n     \"condition\": \"No binding licensing rule is proposed or in force, and the May 2026 guidance has not been withdrawn or relaxed.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 3,\n     \"label\": \"Rule proposed\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule has been proposed, and none is in force.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 4,\n     \"label\": \"Rule in force, partial\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule is in force, and it does not cover every third country outside an exempt list.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 5,\n     \"label\": \"Rule in force, general\",\n     \"condition\": \"A binding licensing rule is in force, and it covers every third country outside an exempt list.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"period_end\"\n  }\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":1,\"Q\":\"holds\",\"O\":\"holds\",\"R\":\"holds\",\"D\":\"holds\",\"X\":\"holds\",\"F\":\"fails\",\"Y\":\"n/a\",\"G\":\"n/a\",\"L\":\"holds\",\"B\":[],\"XR\":\"holds\",\"N\":\"n/a\",\"W\":\"\",\"why\":\"The ranking treats an infringement with any fine but no remedy as better than an infringement with a remedy, even when the fine could be much more damaging.\"}\n{\"item\":2,\"Q\":\"holds\",\"O\":\"holds\",\"R\":\"fails\",\"D\":\"holds\",\"X\":\"holds\",\"F\":\"n/a\",\"Y\":\"n/a\",\"G\":\"n/a\",\"L\":\"fails\",\"B\":[],\"XR\":\"holds\",\"N\":\"n/a\",\"W\":\"The conditions do not distinguish an escalated restriction that is not a licensing rule from no escalation.\",\"why\":\"A licensing rule that applies by product or end user rather than a named country list can fall into neither in-force rung, and the resulting constraint on NVIDIA's sales is missing from not_judged.\"}\n{\"item\":3,\"Q\":\"holds\",\"O\":\"holds\",\"R\":\"holds\",\"D\":\"holds\",\"X\":\"holds\",\"F\":\"holds\",\"Y\":\"n/a\",\"G\":\"n/a\",\"L\":\"holds\",\"B\":[],\"XR\":\"holds\",\"N\":\"n/a\",\"W\":\"A withdrawn filing is described as no decision, although withdrawal is a distinct outcome for the takeover.\",\"why\":\"No marked rule fails.\"}\n{\"item\":4,\"Q\":\"holds\",\"O\":\"holds\",\"R\":\"fails\",\"D\":\"holds\",\"X\":\"holds\",\"F\":\"fails\",\"Y\":\"n/a\",\"G\":\"n/a\",\"L\":\"holds\",\"B\":[],\"XR\":\"holds\",\"N\":\"n/a\",\"W\":\"\",\"why\":\"A closure with no infringement and no commitments satisfies both the first and second rungs, and the ranking treats any fine without a remedy as better than a remedy even if the fine is more damaging.\"}\n{\"item\":5,\"Q\":\"holds\",\"O\":\"holds\",\"R\":\"holds\",\"D\":\"holds\",\"X\":\"holds\",\"F\":\"n/a\",\"Y\":\"n/a\",\"G\":\"n/a\",\"L\":\"fails\",\"B\":[],\"XR\":\"holds\",\"N\":\"n/a\",\"W\":\"The conditions do not distinguish an escalated restriction that is not a licensing rule from no escalation.\",\"why\":\"The resulting constraint on NVIDIA's sales to the affected markets is missing from not_judged.\"}"}}
